---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { CHECKED, VAT } from "../lib/costs";
---

<Base
  title="Method and sources"
  description="How Property Desk calculates transaction costs, what it assumes, and what it deliberately does not tell you."
  path="/method"
>
  <div class="col" style="padding-block: var(--space-l) var(--space-xl);">
    <h1>Method and sources</h1>

    <h2 style="margin-top: var(--space-l);">Where the figures come from</h2>
    <p style="margin-top: var(--space-s);">
      Statutory charges, the transfer fee, mortgage registration and title deed
      issuance, are the Dubai Land Department&rsquo;s published rates. The
      registration trustee fee is the standard tariff, which has a lower band
      below AED 500,000. VAT is applied at {(VAT * 100).toFixed(0)}% to the
      services that carry it, and not to the statutory fees, which do not.
    </p>
    <p>
      Agency commission, the bank arrangement fee, the valuation and the
      developer&rsquo;s no objection certificate are market practice rather than
      published rates. Those are shown as a midpoint of the usual range and
      marked as estimates, because presenting a negotiated fee as a fixed one
      would be the same mistake this site exists to correct.
    </p>
    <p>All figures were last checked in {CHECKED}.</p>

    <h2 style="margin-top: var(--space-l);">The valuation rule</h2>
    <p style="margin-top: var(--space-s);">
      A lender advances against the lower of its own valuation and the price
      agreed. The calculator applies that rule rather than assuming the two
      match, because the gap between them is the single most common reason a
      buyer arrives at transfer short of cash.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top: var(--space-l);">What this does not do</h2>
    <p style="margin-top: var(--space-s);">
      It does not tell you what a property is worth, what to buy, or whether to
      buy at all. It has no view on any building, community or developer, and it
      does not carry listings. It answers one question: given a price you have
      already agreed, what will the transaction cost you.
    </p>
    <p>
      It also does not model ongoing costs. Service charges, cooling, insurance
      and mortgage payments all continue after transfer and none of them appear
      here.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top: var(--space-l);">Corrections</h2>
    <p style="margin-top: var(--space-s);">
      Fees change, and a fee table with no date is a fee table that is quietly
      wrong. If a figure here no longer matches what you were charged, that is
      worth telling us, and the date above is how you check whether it has been
      reviewed since.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
